---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'How to Set Up Alipay as a Foreigner (2026): Step-by-Step Guide';
const description = 'Step-by-step guide to setting up Alipay as a foreigner in China. Download, register with international number, verify identity, and link your foreign credit card.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12">
    <header class="mb-12">
      <a href="/en/payment/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline inline-flex items-center gap-1 mb-4">← Payment</a>
      <div class="text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">Payment</div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        How to Set Up Alipay as a Foreigner (2026)
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Let me walk you through this. I've done it myself and helped about a dozen friends do the same. <strong>It takes about 15 minutes</strong>, and you can do the whole thing before you even board your flight.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 1: Download the App</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Go to your app store and search for <strong>"Alipay"</strong>. The developer is "Alipay (Hangzhou) Technology Co., Ltd." — that's the real one. There are some fakes out there so double-check.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li><strong>iOS:</strong> App Store</li>
        <li><strong>Android:</strong> Google Play</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Your phone needs iOS 12+ or Android 8+. If your phone is more than 5–6 years old, you might run into issues.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 2: Register with Your Phone Number</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Open the app and tap "Sign Up." You'll be asked for your phone number. The good news: <strong>you can use your own international phone number.</strong> Pick your country code from the dropdown (United States is +1, UK is +44, etc.), enter your number, and they'll send you a verification code via SMS.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-100 rounded-2xl p-5 mb-8 not-prose">
        <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm font-medium mb-1">💡 Pro tip</p>
        <p class="text-amber-700 text-sm">Before going further, switch the app to English. Go to <strong>Me → Settings → General → Language → English</strong>. This will save you a lot of confusion later.</p>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 3: Verify Your Identity</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        This is where most people get stuck, but it's really not that complicated. Here's what you need:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li>Your valid passport</li>
        <li>A well-lit room (the photo needs to be clear)</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        In the Alipay home screen, search for <strong>"Tour Pass"</strong> — it's a mini-program inside Alipay designed specifically for international travelers. Tap "Get Started" and follow the prompts:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li>Select ID type → choose "Passport"</li>
        <li>Enter your name <strong>exactly</strong> as it appears on your passport. Capitalization matters.</li>
        <li>Enter your passport number. Double-check this.</li>
        <li>Select your nationality from the dropdown.</li>
        <li>Take a clear photo of your passport's info page.</li>
        <li>Do the face scan / facial recognition. Take off your glasses if you wear them.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        The verification is usually instant, but sometimes it takes 24–48 hours if the system decides to take a closer look. I've never had it take more than a few minutes in practice.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 4: Link Your International Card</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Once you're verified, it's time to add a payment method. In the Tour Pass section, tap "Add Card."
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-4">Supported cards:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Visa</strong> — Works</li>
        <li><strong>Mastercard</strong> — Works</li>
        <li><strong>JCB</strong> — Works</li>
        <li><strong>Discover</strong> — Works</li>
        <li><strong>American Express</strong> — Usually doesn't work</li>
        <li><strong>Prepaid cards / virtual cards</strong> — Forget it</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Enter your card number, expiration date, CVV, and billing address. Your bank might send you a security notification — approve it and you're good.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-100 rounded-2xl p-5 mb-8 not-prose">
        <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm font-medium mb-1">💰 What is Tour Pass?</p>
        <p class="text-amber-700 text-sm">Tour Pass is a prepaid wallet for foreign travelers. Minimum top-up is ¥100 (~$14 USD), max balance is ¥2,000 (~$280 USD), and it's valid for 90 days. Unused balance is automatically refunded after 90 days. For most short trips (1–2 weeks), that ¥2,000 limit is plenty if you top up as you go.</p>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 5: Start Paying</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Open Alipay, tap "Pay" (the big button on the home screen), and you'll see a QR code. Here's the thing about Chinese mobile payment: there are two ways to do it.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 not-prose">
        <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Scenario A: You scan the merchant's QR code</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Tap "Scan" → point at the QR code on the counter → enter the amount → confirm.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6 not-prose">
        <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Scenario B: The merchant scans your QR code</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Tap "Pay/Collect" → show your payment QR code to the merchant → they scan it → done.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Scenario B is actually more common in China for smaller purchases. You'll get used to it after the first time.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <div class="bg-brand-50 border border-brand-100 rounded-2xl p-6 not-prose">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Need to know more?</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li><a href="/en/payment/setup-wechat/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline">Also set up WeChat Pay for backup →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en/payment/foreign-cards/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline">Can you use your credit card directly? →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en/payment/payment-guide/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline">Back to the payment overview →</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
